ships no LICENSE file and the GitHub API reports its license as null. Botspot's
companion project BVM (https://github.com/Botspot/bvm) is released under
GPL-3.0, so GPL-3.0 is what this fork applies, on the understanding that it
matches the author's intent for his other work in this space.

Read that as a statement of intent, not as settled fact. Only Botspot can
license the upstream code he wrote. Until he adds a LICENSE file upstream, or
confirms GPL-3.0 in writing:

- The Blackout Secure additions listed in the version history at the top of
  install-wor.sh are offered under GPL-3.0 without reservation.
- The pre-existing upstream code carries no explicit grant. Anyone planning to
  redistribute this fork commercially, or to relicense it, should get that
  confirmation first.

If you are Botspot and GPL-3.0 is not what you want here, please open an issue
on https://github.com/blackoutsecure/wor-flasher and it will be changed.

## Third-party components

This project downloads, but does not redistribute, the following:

- Windows 10/11 ARM64 installation media, retrieved directly from Microsoft's
  own update servers via uupdump (https://uupdump.net). No proprietary
  Microsoft material is included in this repository. The resulting install is
  unlicensed, exactly like a retail Windows ISO, and must be activated with a
  key or a pre-licensed Microsoft account.
- Windows on Raspberry (WoR) PE-based installer, by the WoR project
  (https://worproject.com).
- Raspberry Pi UEFI firmware, by the Pi Firmware Task Force
  (https://github.com/pftf).
- Windows ARM64 drivers for the Raspberry Pi, by the WoR project
  (https://github.com/worproject/RPi-Windows-Drivers).

Each of those is covered by its own license, held by its own authors.

The config.txt bodies in config-templates/ are derived from the Pi Firmware Task
Force reference configuration (https://github.com/pftf/RPi4/blob/master/config.txt).
